-------------------------6.Bitwise Operators ------------------------
Bitwise operators are used to perform operations on individual bits (0s and 1s) of integer values.

In Java, integers are stored in binary form. Bitwise operators work directly on these binary bits.
1. Types of Bitwise Operators
Java has 7 main bitwise operators.

| Operator | Name | Function |
|---|---|---|
| & | Bitwise AND | Returns 1 if both bits are 1 |
| | | Bitwise OR | Returns 1 if at least one bit is 1 |
| ^ | Bitwise XOR | Returns 1 if both bits are different |
| ~ | Bitwise Complement | Reverses all bits |
~n=-(n+1)
| << | Left Shift | Shifts bits to the left |
a<<k    then
a*2^k
| >> | Right Shift | Shifts bits to the right, preserving the sign |
a>>k    then
a/2^k
---------------code-----------------------

class BitwiseOperators {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int a = 5;
        int b = 3;

        System.out.println("Bitwise AND: " + (a & b));

        System.out.println("Bitwise OR: " + (a | b));

        System.out.println("Bitwise XOR: " + (a ^ b));

        System.out.println("Bitwise NOT: " + (~a));

        System.out.println("Left Shift: " + (a << 1));

        System.out.println("Right Shift: " + (a >> 1));

       
    }
}
output:
